  <h1>What to Do After Nursing Home Neglect</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>1. Report to the ombudsman and APS</h2><p>The Long-Term Care Ombudsman (every state has one) investigates complaints and advocates for residents. Adult Protective Services (APS) investigates abuse and neglect. Both are free. File reports immediately — investigations create official records.</p>
  <h2>2. Request medical records immediately</h2><p>Request all medical records including nursing notes, medication administration records, care plans, and incident reports. Nursing home records are sometimes altered after complaints; requesting promptly under HIPAA (you have the right as the resident&rsquo;s authorized representative) creates pressure to preserve them accurately.</p>
  <h2>3. Photograph all injuries</h2><p>Document pressure sores, bruises, and injuries with dated photographs. A nurse or physician&rsquo;s examination and documentation of injuries is also important. These photographs are often the most compelling evidence in nursing home cases.</p>
  <h2>4. Transfer the resident if necessary</h2><p>If the resident is in immediate danger, transfer them. Inform the new facility of the ongoing issues. A transfer does not waive your legal claim — it protects the resident while the legal matter proceeds.</p>
  <h2>5. Consult an elder abuse attorney</h2><p>Many states&rsquo; elder abuse statutes provide attorney fees for prevailing plaintiffs — meaning specialized nursing home attorneys often take cases on a fee-shifting basis with no upfront cost. Consult an attorney early to preserve evidence and meet any notice requirements.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-nursing-home-neglect-claims-work">how neglect claims work</a> and <a href="/guides/nursing-home-misconceptions">common misconceptions</a>.</p>
